# Imperialism and Colonialism: The Scramble for the World

> In 1800 Europeans controlled perhaps a third of the world's land;
> by 1914, more than four-fifths. The century between — powered by
> steam, quinine and machine guns — redrew every map, made and broke
> every economy it touched, and planted the borders, debts and
> grievances that the twentieth century spent itself settling.

## 1. Definitions and Distinctions

[definition]
**Colonialism** is the practice of acquiring full or partial control
over another territory, settling it and exploiting it economically —
a colony is ruled directly. **Imperialism** is the broader policy of
extending a nation's power through domination, whether by formal
colonies, protectorates, spheres of influence or economic leverage
(informal empire — loans, concessions, gunboats).
[/definition]

| Form | Local government | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Colony | none — ruled from the metropole | British India before 1858; Algeria |
| Protectorate | local ruler survives under treaty | Indian princely states; Egypt |
| Sphere of influence | nominal state, exclusive foreign leverage | China's treaty ports |
| Dominion/settler colony | self-governing, settler-majority | Canada, Australia |
| Mandate (post-1919) | administered "on trust" | Middle East under Britain/France |

## 2. Why Europe Conquered: Causes

- **Industrial hunger**: raw materials (cotton, rubber, minerals) and
  captive markets for the factories' surplus; capital seeking returns
  invested in colonial railways and ports.
- **Technology**: steamships and railways (Suez 1869 cut the Europe–India
  run by weeks), telegraph cables, **quinine** against malaria,
  breech-loading and then machine guns — Hilaire Belloc's line,
  "Whatever happens, we have got / the Maxim gun, and they have not."
- **Strategic rivalry**: every seizure provoked a counter-seizure —
  Egypt's shares (1875), the Falklands, Pacific islands — in a
  security-dilemma spiral among states that could not afford to lose.
- **Nationalism and prestige**: colonies as the measure of great-power
  status; the new states (Germany, Italy) arrived late and hungry.
- **Civilising-mission ideology**: the "White Man's Burden" (Kipling),
  France's *mission civilisatrice*, and the Christian missionary
  impulse — self-justifying doctrines that dressed conquest as duty.
- **Social Darwinism**: pseudo-scientific racial hierarchy lending
  empire an air of natural law — the ideology's darkest stratum.

## 3. Africa: The Scramble, 1880–1914

The trigger was Leopold II of Belgium's private grab of the Congo —
a brutal rubber regime that may have killed millions. To prevent war,
**Bismarck convened the Berlin Conference (1884–85)**, which fixed the
rules: effective occupation on the ground won recognition on the map.
No African was present.

- **Britain**: Cairo-to-Cape ambition — Egypt (1882), Sudan (Kitchener
  at **Omdurman, 1898**), Kenya, Nigeria, the Cape; the Cape-to-Cairo
  railway dream; **Rhodes** and Rhodesia.
- **France**: the west-to-east sweep — Senegal to Chad, plus Madagascar
  and enormous Saharan expanses; Fashoda confrontation with Britain
  (1898) ended in France's retreat.
- **Germany**: Tanganyika, Namibia (the Herero-Nama genocide,
  1904–08), Cameroon, Togo.
- **Belgium**: the Congo Free State — Leopold's personal estate until
  international outrage forced a handover (1908).
- **Portugal**: Angola, Mozambique — claims so old the map already
  showed them.
- **Ethiopia** stood alone: **Menelik II** smashed an Italian army at
  **Adwa (1896)**, keeping Africa's only independent flag; **Liberia**,
  founded by freed American slaves, was the other exception.

By 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia remained outside European rule.

## 4. Asia and the Informal Empires

- **India** — the paradigm: Company rule until 1857 (the revolt that
  changed everything), Crown rule after; the Raj drained wealth
  (Naoroji's "drain theory"), built railways to extract, and left an
  English-speaking administrative class that would inherit the
  country.
- **China** — the informal model: **Opium Wars (1839–42, 1856–60)**
  forced treaty ports, Hong Kong, and extraterritorial courts; the
  **Boxer Rising (1900)** was crushed by an eight-nation army; "spheres
  of influence" partitioned the economy without partitioning the
  state. The scramble's logic without the scramble's flags.
- **South-East Asia**: Dutch East Indies (only Java mattered,
  eventually all of it), French Indochina (1862–87), US takeover of
  the Philippines (1899 — after "splendid little war" with Spain).
- **Japan** — the escape: Meiji modernisation after Perry's black
  ships (1853) made Japan the one Asian power to industrialise —
  then to imperialise (Taiwan 1895, Korea 1910), proving the game was
  about machines, not race.
- **Ottoman Empire** — "the sick man of Europe": financial control
  (Ottoman Public Debt Administration), Balkan breakaways, German
  railway concessions — a slow dismemberment informal in style.

## 5. The Economics of Empire

Formal colonies supplied tropical goods and minerals at dictated
prices and bought manufactured goods behind tariff walls — an
international division of labour that **Hobson (1902)** and
**Lenin (1916, "Imperialism: the Highest Stage of Capitalism")**
theorised as capitalism's need for investment outlets. The record is
uneven: railways, law codes and schools came with extraction,
famine policy (India's 1876–78 and 1896–1902 famines during grain
exports) and forced cultivation (Congo rubber, Indochina's rubber).
De-industrialisation of handicraft textiles in India and Egypt;
single-crop dependence planted across the tropics — the colonial
origin of many post-colonial economic troubles.

## 6. Resistance and Response

Everywhere and always: the 1857 Indian Revolt; the Mahdi's jihad in
the Sudan; the Zulu victory at **Isandlwana (1879)**; Abyssinia's
Adwa; the Boxers; Vietnam's scholars; Indochinese and Indian
nationalist parties founded in exile. Empire never conquered without
cost, and the ideologies it inadvertently spread — nationalism,
constitutionalism, the very geography of "India" and "Indonesia" —
became the weapons of its own destruction.

## 7. Fall and Legacy

- World War I broke the German and Ottoman empires into League of
  Nations **mandates**; World War II bankrupted the imperial powers
  and discredited the racial hierarchies on which rule had rested.
- **1947–1960s**: India (1947), China's revolution (1949), Ghana
  (1957, Africa's first), the 1960 "Year of Africa"; Algeria and
  Vietnam required wars the metropoles lost or abandoned.
- Legacy: arbitrary **borders** (Africa's straight lines; the
  Durand Line; the Sykes–Picot Middle East), economic dependence
  structures, settler-minority states, diasporas, and the
  world-language map (English, French, Portuguese, Spanish) — the
  empire's most durable monument.

## 8. Summary

Imperialism was industrial capitalism plus strategic rivalry plus a
self-serving ideology, armed with technologies that made resistance
hopeless for a time. The Berlin Conference (1884–85) codified the
Scramble for Africa; the Opium Wars modelled informal empire in
China; Ethiopia and Japan proved escape was possible. Colonies were
taken for markets, materials and prestige, and were held by machines
and quinine. Their departure — hurried by two world wars — left
behind the borders and debts that still structure the Global South's
politics.
